From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Luke Craig <lacraig3@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 14:19:49 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] plugins: faulting accesses treat a page without a host
 pointer as I/O

vaddr_ranges_fault (patches 0013/0015/0017) tested only TLB_MMIO, then
copied through the host pointer.  On QEMU 11.1.0 probe_access_internal
folds every slow bit into TLB_MMIO and returns NULL host only then, so
this was equivalent.  Upstream 827802a ("Use TLB_FORCE_SLOW not TLB_MMIO
for system plugins", in 11.1.2) returns the raw flags with host NULL
instead: a store to a ROM page (TLB_DISCARD_WRITE) or any other slow page
would memcpy through NULL.  Treat a NULL host as I/O, as syscall-trap.c's
sc_writable already does.  No change on 11.1.0.
---
 plugins/api-system.c | 11 +++++++++--
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/plugins/api-system.c b/plugins/api-system.c
index b9fa9b859ff5574265bb3ff3076d541107654cfb..a4f838394b7c6a13b53950f44f0db4ede3afd392 100644
--- a/plugins/api-system.c
+++ b/plugins/api-system.c
@@ -454,8 +454,15 @@ static int vaddr_ranges_fault(VaddrRange *r, size_t n,
                     }
                     continue;
                 }
-                if (flags & TLB_MMIO) {
-                    /* Not RAM: an access could have device side effects. */
+                if ((flags & TLB_MMIO) || !host) {
+                    /*
+                     * Not RAM: an access could have device side effects.
+                     * Test the host pointer too: from QEMU 11.1.2 on
+                     * (827802a, TLB_FORCE_SLOW), probe_access_internal
+                     * returns the slow bits (TLB_DISCARD_WRITE for a ROM
+                     * store ...) as they are with *phost NULL, instead of
+                     * folding them into TLB_MMIO.
+                     */
                     ret = QEMU_PLUGIN_VADDR_IO;
                     break;
                 }
